###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# MACE-family models were trained on NVIDIA A100 GPUs; the paper reports
# relative cost only (PAN+PGS adds about 5% inference FLOPs and 1.03-1.05x
# parameters over baseline MACE; Table S14).

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - The PAN and PGS modules are a modification inside the scalar readout pathway of an existing architecture; the corrected models are encoded under the same registry methods (MACE, NequIP, Allegro) with a paper-local 'scalar-pathway' setting, in line with METHODS.md rule 3. If the modification becomes a named method in the literature it will need registry rows.
# - Cutoff, radial basis, irreps and depth are 'held fixed' across variants but their values were not captured; the AdamW settings are stated for the MACE family and are not assumed for Allegro and NequIP.
# - numConfigurations are the rounded totals given in the text (4.6, 3.4 and 3.9 x 10^4), before the 80/10/10 split. The reference level of the public silicon data is not restated.
# - Not encoded: standard deviations over the five seeds, stress MAEs, the PAN-only and PGS-only ablations, capacity-matched controls, the grouped-split check, rMD17 / MD17 results (Tables S2, S3), class-resolved Ag errors, and the MD and physical-property tests.
# - Preprint only (arXiv v1, June 2026); authors from DataCite, without ORCIDs.
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: datacite).
